  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nSee https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/10404#discussion_r1687218279 for context.\r\n\r\nWhen attaching a declarative shadow root, if there is an error (for example, the tag name isn't valid, which the spec says results in a NotSupportedError DOMException, the spec says that the exception will be reported (i.e., an `error` event will be fired at the global object), but this doesn't seem to happen in any browser I tested. The error is logged in the console in Chrome, but no `error` event is fired.\r\n\r\nhttps://excited-jealous-saturn.glitch.me/declarative-shadow-root.html was my test page (trying to distinguish which global it is fired at), but a simpler example is:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nwindow.onerror = (ev) => console.log(\"Window got onerror!\", ev);\r\ndocument.body.setHTMLUnsafe(`<strong><template shadowrootmode=open>!</template></strong>`);\r\n```",
